2026-01-14 19:58:06,354 - INFO - [Router] 问题: 2.	Using published ternary phase diagrams for the Ni–Al–Cr system, investigate the effect on the solid-state equilibria of increasing the Cr content from zero to 25 at%, in a series of alloys containing (i) a constant Al content of 15 at% and (b) a constant Ni content of 75 at%.
答案:
Based on the provided context data, a detailed investigation of solid-state equilibria in the Ni–Al–Cr system as Cr content increases cannot be fully performed, as the actual ternary phase diagrams or equilibrium phase fields are not included. However, key insights can be synthesized from referenced relationships and alloy behaviors. For a series with **constant Al at 15 at%** and increasing Cr (0 → 25 at%), the system transitions from a binary Ni–Al alloy toward a ternary Ni–Al–Cr composition. The Ni–15Al alloy is known to consist of a γ (fcc) matrix with γ′ (Ni₃Al, L1₂) precipitates [source: Temporal evolution, coarsening behavior and oxidation resistance of Ni–15Al superalloy Page unknown Block unknown]. As Cr is added, it partitions primarily into the γ matrix and reduces the γ′ volume fraction, as higher Cr content is associated with a smaller γ′ fraction . Additionally, Cr lowers the γ/γ′ lattice misfit and decreases the γ/γ′ partition ratio of Cr itself, indicating reduced solubility contrast between phases [source: Influence of Cr addition on microstructure of a 5% Re-containing single crystal nickel-based superalloy Page unknown Block unknown]. At sufficiently high Cr levels (>~13 wt%, or ~20 at%), α-Cr phase may precipitate from the γ matrix during aging, especially if the alloy is not rapidly cooled after solution treatment [source: Effects of long term thermal exposure on microstructure and mechanical property evolution of a new wrought Ni–Fe based superalloy Page unknown Block unknown]. Thus, increasing Cr destabilizes the γ′ phase and promotes formation of Cr-rich phases. For a series with **constant Ni at 75 at%** and increasing Cr (with Al decreasing accordingly), the baseline Ni75Al18Cr7 superalloy already contains 7 at% Cr and exhibits an L1₂-strengthened microstructure [source: Microscopic phase-field study on L12 interface properties of Ni75Al18Cr7 superalloy Page unknown Block unknown]. Further Cr addition while holding Ni fixed implies a reduction in Al, which directly diminishes the driving force for γ′ (Ni₃Al) formation, since Al is the primary γ′-forming element [source: The-Superalloys-Fundumentals-and-Applications-81-120 Page 79 Block page_17_block_2]. This shift would reduce γ′ volume fraction and may eventually suppress γ′ entirely at low Al contents. Concurrently, excess Cr beyond its solubility limit in γ will favor α-Cr precipitation, particularly under slow cooling or aging conditions [source: Precipitation and transformation of highly dispersed α-Cr phase in Ni3Al-based superalloy Page unknown Block unknown]. The use of Al-Ni-Cr phase diagrams in conjunction with EDS is standard practice for interpreting such phase equilibria [source: Reactive air aluminizing of a nickel-based superalloy (IN738LC): Coating formation mechanism Page unknown Block unknown], but without explicit diagram data, only qualitative trends can be inferred. In summary, increasing Cr content in both compositional series reduces γ′ stability and promotes α-Cr phase formation, with the effect being more pronounced when Al content is simultaneously reduced.
